1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:02,100
Planning space missions is hard.

2
00:00:02,620 --> 00:00:06,070
Satellites must take the right photos without draining a battery.

3
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:09,430
Constellations must cover every place with few satellites.

4
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:11,510
Experts spend years on these solvers.

5
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:13,650
Can an AI work like a scientist?

6
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:20,000
Give Meta Muse Wonder a hard problem inside an autonomous research loop, and it finds a better solution on its own.

7
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:23,630
Epoch Scientist puts Muse inside an autonomous discovery loop.

8
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:27,130
Muse proposes ideas, writes the code, and learns from scores.

9
00:00:27,180 --> 00:00:30,700
It gets a problem, a simulator, a locked scorer, and a start program.

10
00:00:30,750 --> 00:00:32,920
No internet, papers, or expert code.

11
00:00:32,970 --> 00:00:34,400
Each cycle tests one idea.

12
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:36,630
Muse keeps it only if it scores better.

13
00:00:37,830 --> 00:00:42,110
Then we test once on sealed missions, against expert solvers Muse never saw.

14
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:50,410
AstroAgentBench, an open benchmark, supplies the missions and an independent verifier.

15
00:00:51,050 --> 00:00:56,470
Problem one: photograph 31 places at least every 8 hours, with as few satellites as possible.

16
00:00:56,520 --> 00:01:00,700
On a sealed mission, the start program uses 20 satellites, and misses one place.

17
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:02,350
The expert solver needs 15.

18
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:04,180
Muse needs 10, all on time.

19
00:01:05,550 --> 00:01:08,430
Across five sealed missions, Muse beat the expert on four.

20
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:10,630
On 20 fresh missions, it won 18.

21
00:01:11,450 --> 00:01:15,010
It also traced a score of zero to its own sign error, and fixed it.

22
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:20,230
Problem two: schedule nearly 4,000 photo requests.

23
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:22,770
If one battery hits zero, the plan is invalid.

24
00:01:22,820 --> 00:01:25,650
On a hard mission, the start program empties three batteries.

25
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:29,150
Muse's plan stays valid, and scores 77.9.

26
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:36,440
It beat the start program on 23 of 23 sealed missions, and closed 67% of the gap to the best-known schedule.

27
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:39,030
The loop matters.

28
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:42,090
Three of Muse's first four attempts scored below the start.

29
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:44,230
It improved by failing, and learning.

30
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:45,650
It wrote its own code.

31
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:48,030
But it has limits.

32
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:49,540
Experts still schedule better.

33
00:01:49,590 --> 00:01:51,570
The best agent uses fewer satellites.

34
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:53,060
Each problem had one run.

35
00:01:53,110 --> 00:01:59,340
Give Meta Muse Wonder a hard problem inside an autonomous research loop, and it finds a better solution on its own.
